    "summary": "Solid second‑set jams, Birdsong shines, Brent’s keys standout",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a soaring Bird Song and a thrilling Help On The Way → Slipknot! → Franklin's Tower sequence. Brent Mydland’s keyboard work on Blow Away stands out, while the first set is uneven but recovers with tight second‑set improvisations.",
    "review": "October 22, 1989 at Charlotte Coliseum captures a Dead show that feels like a late‑tour stretch – the first set wobbles a bit before the band finds its groove. Jerry Garcia rides a “Wolf” guitar with upbeat joy, delivering melodic solos on Queen Jane Approximately and keeping the energy high throughout. Brent Mydland shines on keyboards, especially during Blow Away, and even steps up vocally on that number, though a few lyric slips are noted. Phil Lesh’s bass is solid but less front‑and‑center than in earlier years. The highlight reel lives in the second set: the Help On The Way → Slipknot! → Franklin's Tower suite launches into an expansive post‑Franklin jam that rolls through Jam, Drums and Space before soaring into I Need A Miracle, Stella Blue and Throwing Stones. Bird Song glows in the first set, and the encore Black Muddy River provides a gentle close.\n\nAudio wise, the Charlie Miller‑engineered soundboard (gd1989-10-22.sbd.walker-scotton.miller.83319.sbeok.flac16) offers ultra‑clean, crystal‑clear fidelity that captures every nuance of the band’s dynamics, making it the recommended listening source for both newcomers and seasoned Deadheads.",
